Resend a control-channel packet until it is acknowledged, with a capped, bounded backlog.

A reliable packet was resent every 10 ms, at most 20 times, then dropped. On TV Wi-Fi a stall
over ~200 ms (a background scan or power save, every few minutes) used up all 20 resends and
the packet was lost. On the control channel that packet carries HID input: a lost one blocks
the host's ordered channel or desyncs the controller, which freezes input. Steam's own client
resends until the packet is acknowledged, which is why a Steam Deck on the same Wi-Fi doesn't
freeze.

Control-channel reliable packets now keep retransmitting until acknowledged or the session ends
(IHS_RetransmissionKeepGoing), with backoff that only applies to the control channel - 10 ms
doubling, capped at 200 ms (IHS_RetransmissionBackoffMs); other channels keep the old fixed
10 ms over 20 attempts. retransmitCount saturates at 255 instead of wrapping, which would reset
the backoff.

Three things keep "until acknowledged" from running away:

- A cap on the control backlog (CONTROL_PENDING_CAP, 512 packets, ~2 s of HID at 250 Hz). The
  10 s host-silence timeout only fires when nothing arrives at all, but a dead uplink with a
  live downlink (video still playing) leaves it dormant while the queue, its timers and buffers
  grow forever and flush as a burst on recovery. At the cap a HID report is dropped (an old
  delta isn't worth replaying - the host rebuilds from a full report + CRC) and a resync is
  requested; a control message that must be delivered instead disconnects with a Warn, rather
  than being dropped silently. The cap keeps pending well under 32768, which the cumulative-ACK
  wraparound compare (0025) relies on.

- A zombie guard. A resend is handed to the send thread, which re-queues a fresh pending; an ACK
  landing in between marked only the old pending, so the new one would resend forever. Both
  IHS_RetransmissionQueue and the timer now drop a packet a cumulative ACK has already covered
  (the per-channel acked id from 0025).

- When HID reports are dropped at the cap, the next batch sent once the uplink recovers carries
  full reports (IHS_SessionHIDSendReport), so the host resyncs promptly.

Session teardown still frees pending items: the timer is destroyed first, running each task's end
callback, then Deinit frees the rest. The backlog gauges (controlPending, controlMaxRetransmit,
hidBacklogDropped) feed the diagnostics in 0024.

